update public.curriculum_units as c set description = v.description from (values
('0a764ab2-c067-4833-9272-6c6164afa7ba'::uuid, 'The peripheral nervous system connects the central nervous system to receptors, muscles and glands. In this lesson, you will distinguish sensory and motor neurones, describe the roles of nerves and explain how signals travel between the central nervous system and the rest of the body.'),
('1ca0be80-c13b-4c19-9a79-0da4638a0b2a'::uuid, 'Hormones are chemical messengers released by endocrine glands into the bloodstream. In this lesson, you will identify major endocrine glands, give examples of the hormones they produce and explain how hormones act on target organs to regulate processes such as growth, metabolism and reproduction.'),
('1e821b2f-0516-4b5b-9538-402a2f521918'::uuid, 'Not all coordination depends on rapid nerve impulses. Endocrine glands release hormones into the blood, allowing chemical messages to influence target organs over time. This section examines hormonal coordination, the roles of major endocrine glands and how hormones regulate body processes.'),
('1fe232e2-60cb-45e4-8d3f-d96c8b3906ae'::uuid, 'Nervous coordination provides rapid communication, while hormones provide another way to regulate activities across the body. This theme introduces endocrine glands and hormones, focusing on how chemical messages travel in the blood and act on specific target tissues.'),
('25105875-17f2-4232-a3b3-89a7132f9d6d'::uuid, 'Information from sense organs is processed and coordinated by the central nervous system. In this lesson, you will identify the brain and spinal cord, describe their protective structures and outline the roles of major brain regions. You will explain how the central nervous system helps coordinate voluntary and involuntary activities.'),
('2f0bfbcc-da1f-4e81-bdc4-5369a38e43f6'::uuid, 'Sense organs contain receptors that detect particular kinds of stimuli. In this lesson, you will compare the eye, ear, nose, tongue and skin as sensory structures, identify the types of stimuli they detect and explain how receptor signals contribute to an appropriate response.'),
('33255bda-e7bc-4ae8-8dfd-038cec1cbd58'::uuid, 'Neurones transmit information through the nervous system, while synapses control how signals pass between cells. In this lesson, you will identify the main parts of a neurone, relate their structure to impulse transmission and explain how neurotransmitters carry signals across a synapse.'),
('5da4eb70-1757-4ec5-a0d3-da533d68782f'::uuid, 'Nervous coordination begins when receptors detect a change in the internal or external environment. This section examines the main sense organs and how specialised receptor cells respond to light, sound, chemicals, pressure and temperature. You will connect the structure of each organ with the type of information it detects.'),
('5f86e600-bd80-452f-8ac4-e252e0e1c1e7'::uuid, 'Some responses must happen quickly to reduce the risk of injury. Reflex actions provide rapid, automatic responses to particular stimuli, often without waiting for conscious decision-making. This section examines reflex arcs and the pathway through which sensory information leads to a response by an effector.'),
('617cb2b4-4cab-4340-9695-594d6382a3fe'::uuid, 'After receptors detect a stimulus, some information is passed through a short pathway that produces a rapid response. This theme focuses on reflex actions, the roles of the neurones involved and the sequence of events in a reflex arc.'),
('75a96888-136c-47bd-8196-e5945d450ee9'::uuid, 'The body must detect changes in its surroundings and coordinate appropriate responses. The nervous system provides rapid communication through electrical impulses, linking sense organs with the brain, spinal cord and muscles. This section examines the organisation of the nervous system, how nerve cells transmit information and how the body responds to stimuli.'),
('86bd403e-2f96-444b-93b7-bb7693cb628d'::uuid, 'Nervous coordination depends on specialised cells that carry information and communicate with one another. This section examines the structure of neurones and how impulses pass along them, then considers how synapses allow communication between neurones or between a neurone and an effector.'),
('c41b6cd9-d5fa-4bb5-b45d-4d56546235af'::uuid, 'A reflex action is a rapid, automatic response to a stimulus. In this lesson, you will trace a reflex arc from receptor through sensory neurone, relay neurone and motor neurone to the effector. You will explain the value of reflexes in protection and compare them with voluntary actions.'),
('e1d8f75d-f215-4c75-b3f3-8b46741cadca'::uuid, 'The nervous system can coordinate a response only after a stimulus has been detected. This theme introduces the sense organs and their receptors, showing how specialised structures convert environmental changes into signals that can be interpreted by the nervous system.'),
('e6131349-cad3-4f47-a430-50998cdb23a5'::uuid, 'The central nervous system is the main coordinating centre for nervous activity. This section focuses on the brain and spinal cord, explaining how they receive information, interpret it and organise responses. You will relate the main regions of the brain and the spinal cord to their roles in controlling body activities.'),
('f4cf64f6-62ec-493e-9c16-309999e8c06b'::uuid, 'Once the central nervous system has processed information, messages must travel between it and the rest of the body. This section examines the peripheral nervous system, including the nerves that carry sensory information towards the central nervous system and motor commands to effectors.')
) as v(id,description) where c.id=v.id and c.description is distinct from v.description;
